<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Landed since the curator&rsquo;s last approval</h2>
<p>One item, not yet read. The curator read items 30 and 31 at 23:20 UTC on 7 November 2026, and no comment since marks a later item read. Item 34 landed on 8 November, in two commits at 15:32 and 15:38 UTC, the second with the fixes after a cold read. It is a new rule. The next landed change comes as item 35.</p>
<section id="l34" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 34 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;Pages&rdquo;, and skill-rewrite.md, &ldquo;The method&rdquo;, step 7</span>An item not yet read shows the current text, and a later change folds into it</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, against the text before the change; underlined, the words that came in. No words of that text went.</span>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;Pages&rdquo;, line 204, the bullet on the page of skill drafts and the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes: one sentence added at its end</p>
<ul><li>On the page of skill drafts and the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes, an item of a change that landed holds its new text whole, with the old words shown struck through and the new words marked, and its reason inside the item's Select range. The reason says in plain words what was wrong and what the text now says. It never cites a cold read's flag by its code or number, a judge's ruling by its number, or an item of a task by its number. The item has no collapsed current text.<span class="add"> An item that the curator has not said they read always shows the skill's current text: if a later change touches its passage, fold that change into the item, with its own reason beside the earlier one, in place of a new item.</span></li></ul>
<p class="where-in">references/skill-rewrite.md, &ldquo;The method&rdquo;, step 7, &ldquo;Landed changes&rdquo;, lines 24 to 27: four new bullets, after the bullet on every other item</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="add">A folded item is an item into which a later change was folded (<code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;Pages&rdquo;). It strikes through the text before its first change, marks every word added since, and lists each change's reason in order.</span></li>
<li><span class="add">The curator's word that they read the page up to an item drops a folded item only if the fold was published before the word's time. Otherwise the item stays, with the fold marked.</span></li>
<li><span class="add">If a change touches the passages of two items that the curator has not said they read, fold it into each. Its part outside them gets its own item.</span></li>
<li><span class="add">The one item that a draft's landing fix gets is folded too, if its passage has an item that the curator has not said they read.</span></li>
</ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>A new rule.</strong> At 07:15 UTC on 8 November, on the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes, the curator asked: &ldquo;Why wasn&rsquo;t the page updated to reflect the current state of the skill?&rdquo; Change 39 there showed words that the skill no longer had, while change 51, further down the same page, had rewritten them. The coordinator had kept change 39 as it landed, so that the curator&rsquo;s open threads would stay on it, and had put the fix into a new item. The skill said what a landed item holds, but nothing of a later change to the same passage.</p>
<p>The rule now says: an item that the curator has not said they read always shows the skill&rsquo;s current text. A later change to its passage is folded into that item, with its own reason beside the earlier one, and does not become a new item. The rule stands once, in <code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;Pages&rdquo;. Step 7 of the method gives the form of a folded item. It strikes through the text before its first change, marks every word added since, and lists the reason of each change in order. The curator&rsquo;s word that they read the page up to an item drops a folded item only if the fold was published before the word&rsquo;s time; otherwise the item stays, with the fold marked. A change that touches the passages of two items that the curator has not said they read is folded into each, and its part outside them gets an item of its own. And the one item that a draft&rsquo;s landing fix gets is folded too, if its passage has such an item.</p>
<p>A cold read then fixed the wording. The first commit stated the rule in both files. The second keeps it in <code>SKILL.md</code> alone, writes &ldquo;an item that the curator has not said they read&rdquo; in place of &ldquo;unread item&rdquo;, a term that the skill does not define, and puts the form of a folded item into step 7 in place of the repeated rule.</p></div>
</section>

<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
